WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.687
Lecture eighteen's matrix K takes second

2
00:00:06.782 --> 00:00:09.812
differences on a line of points.

3
00:00:09.812 --> 00:00:13.315
For two points it is two, minus one

4
00:00:13.315 --> 00:00:14.830
minus one, two

5
00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.860
with eigenvalues one and three.

6
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.301
Now take a two by two grid of points.

7
00:00:21.385 --> 00:00:24.476
Second differences in both directions

8
00:00:24.559 --> 00:00:27.066
give this four by four matrix

9
00:00:27.066 --> 00:00:28.904
four on the diagonal

10
00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:31.410
minus one for each neighbour.

11
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.411
Can you guess its four eigenvalues

12
00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:36.594
from the one and the three?

13
00:00:36.594 --> 00:00:37.920
Pause and guess.

14
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.107
The tool is the Kronecker product.

15
00:00:45.107 --> 00:00:48.608
A Kronecker B is a block matrix

16
00:00:48.608 --> 00:00:50.836
in every entry of A

17
00:00:50.836 --> 00:00:55.186
put that entry times the whole matrix B.

18
00:00:55.186 --> 00:00:58.581
If A is m by n and B is p by q

19
00:00:58.581 --> 00:01:01.340
the result is m p by n q.

20
00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.691
For example, one, two, three, four

21
00:01:04.691 --> 00:01:07.350
Kronecker zero, one, one, one.

22
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.351
The top left block is one times B

23
00:01:10.351 --> 00:01:13.438
the top right block is two times B

24
00:01:13.438 --> 00:01:14.810
and so on down.

25
00:01:15.624 --> 00:01:18.697
One rule does most of the work.

26
00:01:18.697 --> 00:01:22.267
A Kronecker B, times C Kronecker D

27
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.844
equals A C Kronecker B D.

28
00:01:24.943 --> 00:01:27.521
It is block multiplication

29
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.504
from Lecture three.

30
00:01:29.854 --> 00:01:32.492
It gives the inverse at once

31
00:01:32.583 --> 00:01:35.403
the inverse of A Kronecker B is

32
00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:38.223
A inverse Kronecker B inverse.

33
00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:41.134
Transposes factor the same way.

34
00:01:41.484 --> 00:01:44.247
This matters because sizes multiply.

35
00:01:44.324 --> 00:01:46.779
Two matrices of size one hundred

36
00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:49.466
by one hundred give a product with

37
00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.691
one hundred million entries.

38
00:01:51.768 --> 00:01:53.610
The rules let us compute

39
00:01:53.687 --> 00:01:55.375
with the small pieces

40
00:01:55.375 --> 00:01:57.524
and never form the big one.

41
00:01:58.104 --> 00:02:01.599
The partner of the Kronecker product is vec

42
00:02:01.678 --> 00:02:03.982
which stacks the columns of a

43
00:02:04.061 --> 00:02:06.286
matrix into one long vector.

44
00:02:06.286 --> 00:02:09.304
First column on top, then the second.

45
00:02:09.654 --> 00:02:13.943
Then any product A X B, as a long vector

46
00:02:13.943 --> 00:02:18.232
is B transpose Kronecker A, times vec X.

47
00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:22.731
A matrix acting on both sides of X becomes

48
00:02:22.836 --> 00:02:25.974
one matrix acting on a vector.

49
00:02:26.324 --> 00:02:28.041
Why B transpose?

50
00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:31.260
Column j of the product A X B

51
00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:34.049
mixes the columns of A X

52
00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:37.590
with weights from column j of B.

53
00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:42.204
And column j of B is row j of B transpose.

54
00:02:42.784 --> 00:02:46.069
The formula people remember instead

55
00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.623
the product A times X times

56
00:02:48.715 --> 00:02:52.455
B gives A Kronecker B, in the same order.

57
00:02:52.455 --> 00:02:53.824
Is that right?

58
00:02:54.404 --> 00:02:58.424
Take X equal to one, zero, two, one.

59
00:02:58.424 --> 00:03:03.002
Then A X B is two, seven, four, fifteen

60
00:03:03.002 --> 00:03:07.691
and its vec is two, four, seven, fifteen.

61
00:03:07.691 --> 00:03:12.604
B transpose Kronecker A gives exactly that.

62
00:03:12.954 --> 00:03:17.767
A Kronecker B gives four, five, ten

63
00:03:17.767 --> 00:03:19.103
thirteen.

64
00:03:19.103 --> 00:03:23.114
Wrong, with no error message.

65
00:03:23.464 --> 00:03:24.726
One more trap

66
00:03:24.726 --> 00:03:27.701
NumPy flattens a matrix by rows

67
00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:28.873
not columns.

68
00:03:28.873 --> 00:03:30.586
With rows stacked

69
00:03:30.586 --> 00:03:34.553
the factor becomes A Kronecker B transpose.

70
00:03:34.643 --> 00:03:37.077
Both formulas are right for

71
00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:39.060
their own convention.

72
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.224
Mixing them is the bug.

73
00:03:41.804 --> 00:03:43.835
Here is what vec buys.

74
00:03:43.927 --> 00:03:47.805
The Sylvester equation A X plus X B equals

75
00:03:47.897 --> 00:03:50.483
C appears in control theory.

76
00:03:50.575 --> 00:03:53.068
X is multiplied on the left

77
00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.730
and on the right

78
00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:57.684
so it is not a standard system.

79
00:03:58.034 --> 00:04:02.648
With vec, A X is I Kronecker A times vec X

80
00:04:02.756 --> 00:04:06.190
and X B is B transpose Kronecker

81
00:04:06.297 --> 00:04:07.799
I times vec X.

82
00:04:07.799 --> 00:04:08.872
Add them

83
00:04:08.872 --> 00:04:13.594
and it is one ordinary four by four system.

84
00:04:13.944 --> 00:04:15.807
For these matrices

85
00:04:15.807 --> 00:04:19.924
solving it gives X equals one, two, zero

86
00:04:19.924 --> 00:04:21.002
minus one.

87
00:04:21.002 --> 00:04:23.845
We built C from that answer

88
00:04:23.845 --> 00:04:25.904
so you can check it.

89
00:04:26.484 --> 00:04:28.558
Now the eigenvalues.

90
00:04:28.558 --> 00:04:33.121
If A x equals lambda x and B y equals mu y

91
00:04:33.121 --> 00:04:37.579
the mixed product rule says A Kronecker B

92
00:04:37.579 --> 00:04:39.757
times x Kronecker y

93
00:04:39.757 --> 00:04:43.283
is lambda mu times x Kronecker y.

94
00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:45.564
Eigenvalues multiply.

95
00:04:45.914 --> 00:04:48.068
For the Kronecker sum

96
00:04:48.068 --> 00:04:51.398
A Kronecker I plus I Kronecker B

97
00:04:51.398 --> 00:04:55.609
the same eigenvectors give lambda plus mu.

98
00:04:55.609 --> 00:04:57.274
Eigenvalues add.

99
00:04:57.624 --> 00:05:01.157
And the grid matrix from the start is exactly

100
00:05:01.235 --> 00:05:03.826
K Kronecker I plus I Kronecker K

101
00:05:03.904 --> 00:05:06.495
differences down the columns plus

102
00:05:06.574 --> 00:05:08.772
differences across the rows.

103
00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.891
So its eigenvalues are the

104
00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:14.424
sums of one and three: two, four, four, six.

105
00:05:14.774 --> 00:05:16.814
On a three by three grid

106
00:05:16.895 --> 00:05:20.240
the nine eigenvalues are all the pairwise

107
00:05:20.322 --> 00:05:23.177
sums of the three line eigenvalues.

108
00:05:23.177 --> 00:05:25.706
Fast Poisson solvers use this

109
00:05:25.706 --> 00:05:29.214
they only ever work with the small matrix.

110
00:05:29.794 --> 00:05:32.509
A Kronecker product is a matrix

111
00:05:32.597 --> 00:05:34.085
of scaled copies.

112
00:05:34.173 --> 00:05:36.800
The mixed product rule factors

113
00:05:36.888 --> 00:05:39.077
inverses and eigenvalues

114
00:05:39.077 --> 00:05:42.668
vec turns matrix equations into systems

115
00:05:42.668 --> 00:05:46.434
and the grid Laplacian is a Kronecker sum.

116
00:05:46.784 --> 00:05:50.292
Next: other ways to combine spaces.

117
00:05:50.292 --> 00:05:52.196
Sums of subspaces

118
00:05:52.196 --> 00:05:56.104
and arrays with more than two indices.
